A Lamp for the Ledger

The lighthouse keeper kept a ledger of every ship that passed, though no one had asked him to and no one ever read it.

Each morning he climbed the hundred and twelve steps with a pot of tea in one hand and the ledger under his arm, and each evening he climbed them again to light the lamp.

In the spring a boy from the village began to bring the post on Thursdays. He was eleven, and he wanted to know what the ledger was for.

The keeper said it was for remembering. The boy said that the ships remembered themselves, and the keeper allowed that this was probably true.

Still, the ledger grew. It held the names of trawlers and ferries, of a tall ship that came by once in a gale, and of a small blue yacht that passed every summer with the same two people on board.

By the third summer the boy was reading the ledger aloud while the keeper polished the lens. He read the names in the voice of a man announcing guests at a wedding.

When the blue yacht did not come that year, they waited for it through August and into September. The boy wrote its name at the foot of the page anyway, with a question mark beside it.

In October a letter came from a harbour town in the south. The two people on the blue yacht had grown old, it said, and had sold her, and they wanted to thank whoever it was who waved from the lighthouse every summer.

The keeper read the letter twice and put it inside the cover of the ledger. The boy asked whether he would write back.

He did, that winter, in the slow round hand he used for the ledger. He told them the names of the ships that had passed that year, and said that the lamp was lit every evening, and that it would be lit the next year too.

The boy posted the letter on a Thursday. On the way down the hundred and twelve steps he counted them aloud, the way the keeper did, so that he would not lose his place.

